{#- _partials/_load_counts.py.j2 — persist Rows_Read / Rows_Written (p37 R1.1, R1.11, R1.13).

    Writes ONE state row per include site:

        (run_id, "load_metrics") = {
            run_id, step_name, pipeline_id, original_pipeline_id,
            rows_read: int, rows_written: int, write_confirmed: bool,
            source: "glue_job", timestamp: iso8601,
            rows_deduplicated: int,   # only when dedup is on — see below
        }

    Ints, strings and bools only — no floats, no nested maps. That is
    LGR-9d1c0e4-1's lesson (a bare `except` swallowed a Decimal
    serialisation error and silently dropped a metric every run) applied at
    the producer instead of at the serialiser. `int(...)` and `bool(...)`
    are explicit at every value site so no Spark/py4j numeric can arrive as
    a float.

    WRITE SHAPE IS PutItem, AND THE DECIDING FACT IS IAM. The per-pipeline
    Glue execution role grants `dynamodb:PutItem` ONLY on
    agentic_data_ingestion_state (create_glue_pipeline/iam_roles.py:148-157,
    the grant LGR-7c18e81-1 added). `update_item` on the Run_Pipeline_Row
    would be AccessDenied; `put_item` on it would clobber job_completion's
    terminal status write. A NEW row is the only option — and no IAM change
    means no Foundation deploy.

    BEST-EFFORT (R1.13). Wrapped in `except Exception -> logger.warning`,
    matching the DQ state write at glue_job_s3_tables.py.j2's
    "Failed to write DQ results to DynamoDB" arm. A telemetry failure must
    not fail a job that has already written to the target. This is also
    exactly why Load_Outcome's `not_established` is a reachable STEADY STATE
    (p37 risk 3), not a migration artefact.

    BEST-EFFORT IS A TIME BUDGET, NOT ONLY AN EXCEPTION HANDLER, AND THE CLIENT
    IS EXPLICITLY BOUNDED FOR THAT REASON. The `except` above makes a failure
    non-fatal; it does nothing about how LONG the failure takes, and this write
    runs in a Glue job that may sit in a VPC with no route to DynamoDB. That is
    not hypothetical: run-2026-09-23-15-22-35-f826bece did all of its real work
    in ~60 s, then spent ELEVEN MINUTES here before the `except` fired, because
    the default client retried a connect that could never succeed. The job's own
    last words were "Connect timeout on endpoint URL:
    https://dynamodb.us-west-2.amazonaws.com/". Its VPC had interface endpoints
    for Secrets Manager and a gateway endpoint for S3 — which is why the source
    read worked — and neither a DynamoDB endpoint nor a NAT gateway. The main
    route table's `0.0.0.0/0 -> igw-...` looks like egress and is not: a Glue ENI
    has no public IP, so an internet gateway route is a blackhole for it.

    Two costs, and the second is the one that matters. The visible cost is ~11
    minutes of billable DPU per run. The latent one is that Glue kills a job at
    its Timeout (30 min by default here), so a pipeline whose real work takes
    over ~18 minutes would be killed INSIDE this block — reporting a FAILED run
    whose data had already landed in the target. A telemetry write must never be
    able to do that.

    Hence `connect_timeout=3, read_timeout=5, retries={"max_attempts": 0}` on a
    client used for exactly one PutItem of a tiny row. The numbers:

      * `connect_timeout=3` is what actually bounds the blackhole case — an
        unroutable endpoint fails at connect, never at read. In-region DynamoDB
        connects in single-digit milliseconds, so 3 s is three orders of
        magnitude of headroom for a healthy call.
      * `read_timeout=5` covers the PutItem itself, which is one small item with
        no conditions and no index writes.
      * `max_attempts: 0` is ONE total attempt: botocore reads this key as the
        number of RETRIES and adds one for the initial request
        (`botocore/args.py`, `_compute_retry_max_attempts`), the same trap
        `LGR-b397ee2-4` documents for the Bedrock client. Zero retries is the
        choice this partial's own contract implies — `not_established` is already
        a declared steady state, so a metric lost to a transient blip is within
        the contract, whereas doubling the worst-case delay is not. Left unset,
        the call inherits `_retry.json`'s default of 5 attempts, and DynamoDB's
        own entry raises it further; that inheritance is what produced the 11
        minutes.

    Worst case is now ~3 s instead of unbounded. Note this does NOT make
    DynamoDB reachable from such a VPC — telemetry there still records nothing,
    and `Load_Outcome` still reads `not_established`. Reachability is a
    deployment concern (a DynamoDB gateway endpoint, or a NAT), documented in
    docs/JDBC_SOURCE.md; this block's job is to stop being slow about it.

    NO NEW `.count()` IN THIS PARTIAL. Reuses the `source_count` /
    `transformed_count` locals every template already computes. R1.1 is a
    persistence change, not a computation change, and that still holds here.
    p64 2c.4 does add two counts to the pipeline, but they are emitted by
    `_partials/_dedup.py.j2` inside its own enabled arm and this partial only
    READS the local they leave behind — so a pipeline with dedup off pays for no
    count this partial caused, which is the property the original wording was
    protecting.

    `rows_deduplicated` (p64 R7.6, task 2c.4) IS OMITTED ENTIRELY WHEN DEDUP IS
    OFF, rather than written as a zero. Three reasons, and the first is the one
    that decided it:

      * A zero would change the rendered output of EVERY pipeline, including the
        ones that never opted in. That breaks `tests/fixtures/golden_scripts/`
        (ten stored scripts carry this block) and gives up the no-rendered-bytes
        standard 2c.3 holds itself to. Omission keeps both, and keeps this change
        invisible to a dedup-off render.
      * Readers must already tolerate the key's absence: every row written before
        2c.4 lacks it. So absence is a case the consumer needs regardless, and
        emitting a zero would not remove it — only hide it until the first old
        row is read.
      * A written zero and an absent key are different claims. Zero means "dedup
        ran and removed nothing"; absent means "dedup did not run". Folding the
        two would be a smaller version of exactly the misattribution R7.6 is
        about.

    THE `empty_source` STAGE EMITS THE LITERAL `0`, NOT A READ OF THE LOCAL. That
    site sits inside the early exit, ABOVE the dedup block, so
    `_adi_dedup_dropped` does not exist there — reading it would be a NameError in
    a `try` that swallows it, silently dropping the whole metrics row. Zero rows
    were read, so zero were deduplicated, and the literal states that without
    depending on a local. It is a stage-conditional literal for the same reason
    `rows_written` and `write_confirmed` are: this partial has no defaults, and a
    stage decides the value rather than an optional variable read supplying one.

    THE GUARD IS THE SAME `is mapping and … is true` SHAPE `_dedup.py.j2` USES,
    deliberately, and copied rather than reinvented. `quality_config` is set
    unconditionally in `code_assembly.generate_code`'s `template_vars`, but the
    render sites in the test suite omit it, and `is mapping` is an `isinstance`
    check that consults no sentinel — so it answers False for an unset variable,
    a `None`, a scalar, or a foreign `missing` from a second jinja2 copy in
    `sys.modules`, without raising and without the identity question that broke 18
    tests once. It fails CLOSED in every one of those cases, which for this field
    means the key is omitted rather than a garbage value written. The two partials
    must agree on when dedup ran, so they ask the question the same way.

    NO `df.cache()` / `df.unpersist()`. LGR-0450623-1 records a reviewer's
    `cache()` addition to glue_job_jdbc_source.py.j2's full-table path being
    REVERTED because tests/unit/templates/test_glue_job_jdbc_source.py
    documents and asserts that full-table mode emits neither.

    `pipeline_id` IS CARRIED, DELIBERATELY. Omitting it would make
    execution_updater._process_record drop the row for want of a pipeline —
    a free dodge resting on an omission rather than on a rule. The rule is
    the Step_Row allowlist in execution_updater (p37 C6), which routes this
    row away from the step machinery before any status is derived, so it
    grows no phantom step. Carrying it also keeps the row queryable through
    the PipelineIndex GSI like every other state row.

    REQUIRED CALLER VARIABLE: `load_counts_stage`, one of

      'empty_source' — inside the empty-source early-exit branch, before the
                       exit. rows_written = 0, write_confirmed = False.
                       Without this site R1.3's `empty_source` is
                       UNRECORDABLE: the job is gone before
                       transformed_count exists.
      'post_write'   — after the target write block completed without
                       raising. rows_written = transformed_count,
                       write_confirmed = True.
      'pre_write'    — after transformed_count is computed but BEFORE the
                       write block, for a template whose structure does not
                       allow the post-write position.
                       rows_written = transformed_count,
                       write_confirmed = False.

    The stage is STATED by the caller, never guessed, because
    `transformed_count` is rows handed to the writer, not rows confirmed
    written (p37 risk N1) — and `write_confirmed` is the only place that
    distinction survives into the data. There is deliberately no default:
    with StrictUndefined an omitted stage fails the render loudly rather
    than stamping a `write_confirmed` nobody chose.

    INDENT IS DERIVED FROM THE STAGE, INLINE, AND THERE IS NO OVERRIDE HOOK.
    8 for 'empty_source' (inside `try:` + `if source_count == 0:`) and 4
    otherwise (module body inside `try:`) — the nesting every one of the five
    templates actually has. It is computed IN the `indent()` argument rather
    than via `{% set %}` from an optional `load_counts_indent | default(...)`,
    and that is a fix, not a style preference.

    The `default()` form read a variable NO CALLER EVER SET, so its value came
    from `Undefined` handling — and `default()` decides via
    `isinstance(value, Undefined)`. When a process holds TWO copies of the
    jinja2 package, `Context.resolve_or_missing` returns copy A's `missing`
    sentinel while the compiled template's `is missing` test compares against
    copy B's. `_MissingType` is not an `Undefined` and is truthy, so
    `default(..., true)` returned the foreign sentinel verbatim, `indent`
    received it as its `width`, and codegen died with
    "can't multiply sequence by non-int of type '_MissingType'". Two jinja2
    copies is exactly what `mock.patch.dict("sys.modules", ...)` produces on
    exit — it clears sys.modules and restores its entry snapshot, evicting
    every module first imported inside the block. That is a real condition in
    this repo's test suite (118 modules use that idiom), and it broke all 18
    tests in tests/integration/pipeline_flow/test_code_generation_e2e.py.

    Both branches here are int literals, so `indent`'s width cannot be a
    sentinel regardless of how many jinja2 copies are loaded. If a template
    ever nests differently, give it its own stage rather than reintroducing an
    optional-variable read.

    RUNTIME ARGUMENT RESOLUTION. `_runtime_arg` / `_adi_run_id` /
    `_adi_effective_run_id` come from _partials/_runtime_args.py.j2, which
    resolves ONLY run_id. `pipeline_id` and `state_table` are resolved HERE,
    through the same `_runtime_arg` helper, exactly as the s3_tables DQ
    block does for its own write — the partial does not resolve them.

    The write is gated on the real `--run_id` AND a non-empty pipeline_id,
    the same gate shape the DQ write uses (`if _dq_outcomes and _dq_run_id`).
    A manual "start job" run passes no Arguments, so there is no run to
    attach counts to; and `pipeline_id` is a PipelineIndex GSI key, so an
    empty one would be a ValidationException rather than a warning.
-#}
{%- set _lc_rows_written = '0' if load_counts_stage == 'empty_source' else 'transformed_count' -%}
{%- set _lc_write_confirmed = 'True' if load_counts_stage == 'post_write' else 'False' -%}
{%- set _lc_dedup_on = quality_config is mapping and quality_config.get("dedup_enabled") is true -%}
{%- set _lc_rows_deduplicated = '0' if load_counts_stage == 'empty_source' else '_adi_dedup_dropped' -%}
{%- filter indent(8 if load_counts_stage == 'empty_source' else 4, true) -%}
# p37 R1.1/R1.11: record Rows_Read / Rows_Written for this run
# (stage: {{ load_counts_stage }}). Best-effort telemetry (R1.13) — a failure
# here must NOT fail a job that has already written to the target, so the
# whole block is wrapped like the DQ state write. Reuses the existing
# source_count / transformed_count locals — this block counts nothing itself.
try:
    import boto3 as _adi_lc_boto3
    from botocore.config import Config as _adi_lc_Config
    from datetime import datetime as _adi_lc_dt, timezone as _adi_lc_tz

    # Bounded, single attempt: this job may have no route to DynamoDB, and an
    # unbounded client turns a best-effort metric into minutes of stalled time.
    _adi_lc_ddb_config = _adi_lc_Config(connect_timeout=3, read_timeout=5, retries={"max_attempts": 0})
    # run_id comes from _runtime_args; pipeline_id and state_table are
    # resolved here through the same _runtime_arg helper (_runtime_args
    # resolves run_id only).
    _adi_lc_pipeline_id = _runtime_arg("pipeline_id")
    _adi_lc_state_table = _runtime_arg("state_table", "agentic_data_ingestion_state")
    # int()/bool() are explicit so no float and no py4j numeric can reach
    # DynamoDB — the row is ints, strings and bools only.
    _adi_lc_rows_read = int(source_count)
    _adi_lc_rows_written = int({{ _lc_rows_written }})
    _adi_lc_write_confirmed = bool({{ _lc_write_confirmed }})
{%- if _lc_dedup_on %}
    # p64 R7.6: rows removed by deduplication, so rows_read - rows_written stays
    # decomposable instead of attributing the drop to quarantine or the transform.
    _adi_lc_rows_deduplicated = int({{ _lc_rows_deduplicated }})
{%- endif %}
    if _adi_run_id and _adi_lc_pipeline_id:
        _adi_lc_boto3.resource("dynamodb", config=_adi_lc_ddb_config).Table(_adi_lc_state_table).put_item(
            Item={
                "run_id": _adi_run_id,
                "step_name": "load_metrics",
                # Both spellings, same value, as run_pipeline writes them
                # (run_pipeline/lambda_function.py:161-163).
                # execution_updater prefers original_pipeline_id (:173).
                "pipeline_id": _adi_lc_pipeline_id,
                "original_pipeline_id": _adi_lc_pipeline_id,
                "rows_read": _adi_lc_rows_read,
                "rows_written": _adi_lc_rows_written,
{%- if _lc_dedup_on %}
                "rows_deduplicated": _adi_lc_rows_deduplicated,
{%- endif %}
                "write_confirmed": _adi_lc_write_confirmed,
                "source": "glue_job",
                "timestamp": _adi_lc_dt.now(_adi_lc_tz.utc).isoformat(),
            }
        )
        logger.info(
            f"Load metrics recorded: rows_read={_adi_lc_rows_read}, "
            f"rows_written={_adi_lc_rows_written}, "
{%- if _lc_dedup_on %}
            f"rows_deduplicated={_adi_lc_rows_deduplicated}, "
{%- endif %}
            f"write_confirmed={_adi_lc_write_confirmed}"
        )
    else:
        logger.info(
            "Load metrics not recorded: no --run_id/--pipeline_id "
            "(manual job start) — Load_Outcome reads not_established"
        )
except Exception as _adi_lc_err:
    logger.warning(f"Failed to write load metrics to DynamoDB: {_adi_lc_err}")
{%- endfilter -%}
